The formula $m_{
u} \approx m_D^2 / M_R$ describes the mass of the light physical neutrino, $m_{
u}$, within the Type-I seesaw mechanism. It relates $m_{
u}$ to two other fundamental mass scales. The term $m_D$ represents a Dirac mass, analogous to the masses of other Standard Model fermions, which arises from the Yukawa coupling between a left-handed neutrino, the Higgs field, and a new right-handed sterile neutrino. The mass $m_D$ is naturally expected to be on the order of other fermion masses, such as the electroweak scale. The term $M_R$ is the large Majorana mass of the hypothetical heavy right-handed neutrino, which is a singlet under the Standard Model gauge group. The formula demonstrates an inverse relationship: if $M_R$ is extremely large (e.g., near a Grand Unification scale of $\sim 10^{15}$ GeV), then the observed neutrino mass $m_{
u}$ becomes proportionally tiny (sub-eV scale). This elegant mechanism simultaneously explains the smallness of $m_{
u}$ and predicts the existence of a super-heavy neutrino partner.

