The formula $\partial_\mu J^\mu = 0$ describes the local conservation of a quantity in a relativistic context. The term $J^\mu$ represents the four-current density, a contravariant four-vector. Its time component, $J^0 = ho$, is the density of the conserved quantity (e.g., charge density), and its spatial components, $\vec{J}$, form the three-dimensional current density. The operator $\partial_\mu$ is the four-gradient, $( rac{1}{c} rac{\partial}{\partial t}, \vec{
abla})$. The contraction $\partial_\mu J^\mu$ (summation over $\mu$ is implied) is the four-divergence of the four-current. Setting this to zero expands to the standard continuity equation: $ rac{\partial ho}{\partial t} + \vec{
abla} \cdot \vec{J} = 0$. This guarantees that any local change in density over time, $ rac{\partial ho}{\partial t}$, is exactly balanced by the divergence of the current, $-\vec{
abla} \cdot \vec{J}$.
